> On 2007-11-23, John H. <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Boots of Stepguarding
>
> They need to display a message when they do this and force a --More--;
> otherwise people will bang on the keys and think they're lagged when
> actually the boots are stopping them.

Interesting, but potentially annoying if there are a lot of traps
around.  I'd think a message but no --more-- forcing.


> > These boots do not take the player's state into account,
>
> This will piss me off in combination with my ring of levitation.

I'm thinking that levitation should be taken into account, since it's
a physical state.  The idea is that the boots consider the ground, so
if the player is floating they do not operate.


> Are monsters considered dangerous or not?

Living monsters are never stepped on, so the question is moot.


> > Boots of Running
> This is a neat idea but to be done right would probably require a major
> fiddle/rewrite of the speed system; right now you get a turn every so
> often, depending on your speed, and you can do with it what you want.  
> These boots as written require knowing what you're going to do with
> your turn before you do it; tricky.
>
> The bolt-on hack version would be to just fiddle your speed depending on
> what you just _did_, but even that is a little hairy given that parts of
> the code don't really know what other parts did, etc. etc.  That also
> would alter the concept above slightly.

Ah, good information thanks.  I'd think the bolt-on hack would be the
way to do it.  There's already support for multi-turn actions in the
game, but to do it that way would involve creating fractional delays,
which would probably be tricky.  Worth thinking about....


> > Should not make the player faster than someone who
> > continually jumps with jumping boots,
>
> Why would I wear these again?  :/  Jumping is already one of the best
> escapes because you don't really need to outrun most monsters, you just
> need to break contact with them.

Jumping boots don't work if you can't see the destination square.  (In
any case, I think jumping boots are a little overpowered since the
player can jump repeatedly without penalty.  I'd think a patch that
included running boots would probably nerf jumping boots a little.)


> > but should make his run speed faster than speed boots.  
>
> There's still a debate as to whether speed boots are faster than
> jumping, given that jumping always takes a single turn and speed boots
> will occasionally give you "free" turns.

A successful jump can consistantly carry the player three spaces,
right?  Speed boots can't match that for fleeing.


> I think the most recent "fast ascension" in devnull used speed boots,
> not jumping; wasn't watching, so I don't know for sure.

If the ascension was real-time fast then speed boots are the way to
go, since the player doesn't have to stop, #jump and specify
destination all the time.  If the ascension counted turns, then
jumping boots really don't save all that many turns.  They're useful
for getting away from the Riders more than decreasing turn counts.


> > Boots of Wandering
> Has no effect; skilled players currently BUC-ID all boots before putting
> them on anyway, to avoid the boots of fumbling.  (Also, one in ten moves
> isn't really that much.)

Well the idea of adding new magic item types isn't just to expand a
player's strategic options, but to make the other magic items less
common.  And aren't the really good boot types sometimes generated
cursed as well?  And again, we should remember that Nethack isn't just
a game for skilled players, all kinds still play the game.

One in ten moves not being much: it can be enough if the player's
walking by water or lava, or is being chased by a mumak.  Anyway, a
possibly-cursed item cannot make the game too annoying to play, and
they have to make the player move randomly less often than confusion,
which is already not that bad in generic situations.

